;;; `dead` is NOT upward-closed under `absent` — M1e Q-M1e.9, reproduced
;;; 2026-08-28 and banked here at S1e.2.3.
;;;
;;; ── The premise this refutes ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; design/08 § The objects:
;;;
;;;   dead(X) — X holds a contradiction. Monotone: X ⊆ Y ∧ dead(X) ⇒ dead(Y),
;;;   because the KB is append-only and nothing retracts.
;;;
;;; *Append-only, nothing retracts* makes saturation **inflationary**. It does
;;; not make it **monotone in its input**, and `(absent P)` is exactly what
;;; separates the two — which `absent_semantics.md` C3 already says from the
;;; other direction: removing a fact can flip an absent and *fabricate* a
;;; contradiction the full KB never had.
;;;
;;; Here `bad` fires only while `q` is missing:
;;;
;;;   alive₀          = {(p A), (q A)}
;;;   {(p A)}         is DEAD   — bad fires, (q A) absent
;;;   {(p A), (q A)}  is ALIVE  — the guard's witness is present
;;;
;;; so the single maximal state is {(p A), (q A)} and dead is not upward-
;;; closed. Three shipped mechanisms read the false premise, each sufficient
;;; alone: the lookahead kill cache (writes `(not (p A))` with no premises),
;;; the singleton writeback (design/08 claim (1) on its own terms), and the
;;; width-1 no-good clause (subsumes the live superset, so it is never
;;; generated). Five of the six shipped configurations answer wrongly, and
;;; every one of them reports `exhausted = true`.
;;;
;;; ── What this file is for ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(config :warn-derived-naf true)` turns on the S1e.2.3 diagnostic, so the
;;; **warning is part of this file's recorded output** — that is what makes
;;; the containment a fixture rather than a claim. The rule `bad` is reported
;;; as concluding `(false)` from an `(absent …)` over `q`, which this
;;; program's generator can still propose.
;;;
;;; **Its `:expect` states today's answer and is meant to break.** When
;;; F18 makes the three consumers world-aware — or S1f.10.8 forbids the shape
;;; outright — the model here changes and this file has to be revisited. That
;;; is the same tripwire `alive-empty-{phase1,interlayer}.ein` carry, and the
;;; same reason.
;;;
;;; The goal is `is-a` rather than `p`, because the model the engine reaches
;;; contains no `p` fact at all and an expectation cannot state an answer with
;;; an empty goal extent — Q-M1e.13, the same reason `alive-empty-phase1.ein`
;;; moved its goal.
;;;
;;; ── The user's reading, 2026-08-28 ─────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; > not any well-formed ein program is correct. An example with
;;; > `(rule bad () …)` is broken by design … there is no definition of `q`
;;; > and `p` relations, no obligations, only the rule that asserts `(false)`.
;;;
;;; It is a **mis-encoded obligation**: it says *a world with p and without q
;;; is false* and never says *q is required*, which is what M1d built
;;; `(open ?R)` for. The engine's complaint is not wrong; the word is. That
;;; reading is why the diagnostic names `(open ?R)` as one of its two
;;; replacements — the other being `total`'s stored-negative discipline.

(config :warn-derived-naf true)

(relation is-a T T)
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(is-a A T)

(rule bad ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} has p without q"
  :priority 200)

(query
  :goal   (is-a ?x T)
  ;; Today's answer, and the tripwire: the search records `(q A)` and the
  ;; cached `(not (p A))` and calls that a model. The maximal state is
  ;; `{(p A), (q A)}`, so a world-aware fix drops `(not (p A))` and adds
  ;; `(p A)` — and this line stops holding, which is the point of writing it.
  :expect (model (is-a A T) (q A) (not (p A))))
